{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows that agreed requirement text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop work that depends on the change and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** When implementation shows that agreed requirement text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to:\n1. stop work that depends on the change, and\n2. take the changed requirements through review, agreement and commit before continuing.\n\nThe requirement has no other modes or boundary cases.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_stops_dependent_work_when_agreed_text_must_change]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It makes two kinds of check:\n- `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", …)` runs on the whole bundled skill text. That text is the `SKILL.md` and its reference files, with whitespace collapsed by `collapse`, so a wrapped line still matches. The test asserts the skill contains: \"If implementation shows that agreed text must change, stop work that depends on it and take the changed requirements through steps 3 to 5 first\". `assert_text` panics if the phrase is missing. This covers the trigger and the stop-dependent-work directive. It also covers the instruction to route the change through steps 3 to 5.\n- The test then asserts that `step(\"habeas-requirements\", n)` starts with \"Review.\" for step 3, \"Present\" for step 4 and \"Commit.\" for step 5. Those assertions tie \"steps 3 to 5\" to review, agreement and commit.\n\nI checked this against `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, section Workflow:\n- The sentence appears in the paragraph after the numbered steps.\n- Step 3 is \"Review.\". It is the subagent review.\n- Step 4 is \"Present each group to the user\". It carries the agreement gate.\n- Step 5 is \"Commit.\".\n\n**Mapping.**\n- The trigger is covered by the phrase assertion.\n- \"Stop dependent work\" is covered by the phrase assertion.\n- \"Review, agreement, commit before continuing\" is covered by the phrase \"through steps 3 to 5 first\" plus the step-prefix assertions.\n\n**Possible violations the test would catch.**\n- Dropping or weakening the directive, for example removing \"stop work that depends on it\", fails the phrase assertion.\n- Renumbering or reordering the workflow so steps 3 to 5 no longer mean review, agreement and commit fails the prefix assertions.\n\n**Remaining checks.** I found no relevant unmarked tests and no missing scenarios. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply. The test adequately checks the requirement.",
